    "narrative": {
        "northStar": "The Lab Bench",
        "overview": "The playground is a quiet instrument panel next to a live experiment. The 3D scene is the subject and takes most of the screen. The sidebar holds labelled, calibrated controls and readouts. Nothing in the chrome competes with the motion in the viewport. The design shows in precision instead: numbers that don't jitter, units on every value, and one colour that means \"act here\".\n\nThe system is dense but calm. It uses the platform's own sans-serif for text and a monospace face with tabular digits for every measured value. There is one accent colour and a fixed red/green/blue mapping for the three axes. Surfaces are flat and separated by hairlines. Only things that float over the 3D scene lift off it. Light and dark themes follow the system setting, but the stage itself is always dark, so the physics reads the same in both.",
        "keyCharacteristics": [
            "The scene leads; the sidebar is fixed-width and quiet.",
            "One accent, used only for the primary action, slider fill, the selected option and the focus ring.",
            "X is red, Y is green, Z is blue, in every control, readout and viewport arrow.",
            "Every number is monospaced and tabular, with a unit and a fixed precision.",
            "Flat panels with 1px hairlines; shadows only on overlays above the stage."
        ],
        "rules": [
            {
                "name": "The One Accent Rule",
                "body": "Aether Teal marks one action per group, the selected option and focus. If two things on screen are both teal fills, one of them is wrong.",
                "section": "colors"
            },
            {
                "name": "The Axis Rule",
                "body": "Red is X, green is Y and blue is Z, everywhere, and these hues are never reused for anything else. When a series palette has to sit next to axis colours, label the series as well.",
                "section": "colors"
            },
            {
                "name": "The Dark Stage Rule",
                "body": "The viewport stays dark in both themes. Anything drawn over it uses the HUD tokens, not the page theme.",
                "section": "colors"
            },
            {
                "name": "The Tabular Rule",
                "body": "Every number that changes over time is monospaced with `tabular-nums`, has a fixed precision per quantity, and shows its unit (`m`, `m/s`, `N`, `Hz`, `°`, `s`). A bare or jittering number is a bug.",
                "section": "typography"
            },
            {
                "name": "The Flat Bench Rule",
                "body": "Nothing in the sidebar has a shadow. If a group needs separating, use space or a hairline, not elevation.",
                "section": "elevation"
            }
        ],
        "dos": [
            "Do use a token (`var(--…)`) for every colour in UI code, and add a dark value next to each new one.",
            "Do keep `src/playground/demos/helpers/colors.ts` in step with the dark-theme axis and series tokens, since the stage is always dark. Three.js can't read CSS variables.",
            "Do show every changing number in the data face with a unit and a fixed precision.",
            "Do give every input a visible label or an `aria-label`, and keep the 2px Aether Teal focus ring.",
            "Do put new viewport overlays on the HUD tokens with the stage-overlay shadow."
        ],
        "donts": [
            "Don't add a second accent colour, or fill more than one button per group with Aether Teal.",
            "Don't reuse red, green or blue for anything other than X, Y and Z.",
            "Don't add shadows or tonal cards inside the sidebar. It stays flat.",
            "Don't let the viewport follow the light theme. The stage is always dark.",
            "Don't use coloured edges thicker than 1px except the axis marker on vector inputs."
        ]
    }
}
